("creative", "The last train out of Halden left at 11:52, and Theo was the only passenger. He sat by the window, watching the lights of the town shrink into the dark until they were no bigger than sparks. He had told no one he was leaving. Not his mother, who would be asleep by now with the radio still murmuring beside her bed. Not Lena, who had waited for him at the bridge every evening that summer and would wait again tomorrow. The conductor passed through the carriage without a word, punched his ticket, and disappeared. Theo pressed his forehead to the cold glass. Somewhere ahead was the city, and a life he'd only ever imagined. Behind him, everything he knew was quietly falling away."),
("creative", "Nobody in Willow Creek believed the old Hendricks house was haunted until the lights came on. It had been empty for twenty years, its windows boarded and its garden swallowed by weeds. Then, one cold October night, Mrs. Abernathy saw a soft yellow glow in the upstairs window while walking her dog. By morning, the whole town was talking. Some said it was squatters. Others swore they had heard music drifting from the house, a slow waltz played on a piano that had been out of tune for decades. Twelve-year-old Sam didn't care what the adults thought. He only knew that his grandfather had grown up in that house, and that his grandfather had never once agreed to talk about it."),
